FILE: crates/franken-node/src/connector/rollout_state.rs:368

FINDING: persist_lock(path) keys the in-process registry by persist_lock_path(path).canonicalize().unwrap_or_else(|_| persist_lock_path(path)). Same race as bd-(root_pointer): on the FIRST persist for a given path the .lock file does not yet exist, canonicalize() returns Err, and the registry key is non-canonical. After the .lock file is created (by the open call inside with_persist_locks at line 446), canonicalize() succeeds for subsequent calls and the key changes. Two callers using different but equivalent path spellings (./state.json vs /abs/state.json, or paths with a parent symlink) get DIFFERENT Arc<Mutex<()>> entries from the registry.

The hazard is partially masked by the file-flock acquired first at line 459 -- but Rust std File::lock uses flock(2) on Linux which is per-OFD, so two open() calls in the same process produce two OFDs that can each hold the exclusive lock simultaneously. The in-process mutex is the ONLY thing serializing intra-process writers; if its keying splits, two threads in the same process can both pass the lock pair and concurrently rewrite the rollout state file. ObligationTracker reservations and version bumps are then non-atomic with respect to each other.

SCENARIO:
1. Thread A: with_persist_locks("./rollout.json", ...) -> opens lock fd1, flock-exclusive succeeds. Calls persist_lock("./rollout.json") -> canonicalize fails (lock file may have just been created but FS may still report ENOENT under racy mkdir, or path is genuinely relative). lock_key = "./rollout.json.lock". Inserts M1, takes M1.
2. Thread B (concurrent): with_persist_locks("/abs/rollout.json", ...) -> opens lock fd2, flock-exclusive succeeds (per-OFD). Calls persist_lock -> canonicalize succeeds, lock_key = "/canonical/abs/rollout.json.lock". Inserts M2, takes M2.
3. Both threads enter write_state() concurrently: read current state, reserve obligation in tracker (the tracker is shared via rollout_obligation_tracker but each thread has its own atomic temp-file write), do atomic rename. Last writer wins; first writer's obligation is silently dropped from the on-disk state.

SUGGESTED FIX:
- Drop the canonicalize() and key by canonical(parent_dir).join(lock_file_name). The parent dir exists (callers ensure_dir before persist), so canonicalize is reliable.
- Or pre-touch the lock file before the canonicalize call so the file exists by the time canonicalize runs.
- Add a regression test that calls persist() twice with paths that differ only by symlink/relative-vs-absolute spelling and asserts that the registry is queried with the same key (e.g., expose the registry size).

EVIDENCE:
crates/franken-node/src/connector/rollout_state.rs:368-388
```
fn persist_lock(path: &Path) -> Result<Arc<Mutex<()>>, PersistError> {
    let lock_key = persist_lock_path(path)
        .canonicalize()
        .unwrap_or_else(|_| persist_lock_path(path));   // <-- splits on ENOENT
    persist_lock_registry().lock()...
        .map(|mut locks| {
            locks.retain(|_, weak_lock| weak_lock.upgrade().is_some());
            if let Some(lock) = locks.get(&lock_key).and_then(Weak::upgrade) {
                return lock;
            }
            let lock = Arc::new(Mutex::new(()));
            locks.insert(lock_key, Arc::downgrade(&lock));
            lock
        })
}
```
crates/franken-node/src/connector/rollout_state.rs:441-473 with_persist_locks acquires file flock then process_lock; per-OFD flock semantics on Linux mean intra-process concurrent open()+lock both succeed, so the in-process Mutex is the only safety net.

FILE: crates/franken-node/src/sdk/verifier_sdk.rs:452-467
FINDING: VerifierSdk::verify_artifact() computes the 'expected' artifact hash by SHA256-hashing the artifact_id string itself (deterministic_hash(artifact_id) at line 453: hasher.update(b"verifier_sdk_v1:"); hasher.update(data.as_bytes())) and ct_eq's the user-supplied artifact_hash against THAT, not against the actual artifact bytes. The check is named 'hash_match' and presented as evidence in the report, giving downstream consumers false confidence that artifact integrity has been verified.

WHY IT'S A BUG: The Universal Verifier SDK (VSK) is the public 'stable, versioned API' for third parties to verify artifacts (per module docs lines 1-14). Any caller supplying a real SHA-256 of the artifact contents will FAIL the check unless they happen to also have computed SHA256("verifier_sdk_v1:" || artifact_id). The only way to pass the check is to derive the hash from the ID — meaning the hash field is decorative, not security-relevant. This is a stub: the SDK has no I/O surface to load artifact bytes, so an honest verifier cannot exist here without taking bytes (or a transport handle) as a parameter.

SUGGESTED FIX: Either (a) accept artifact bytes (or a Reader) as a parameter and ct_eq(artifact_hash, sha256(bytes)); (b) move hash verification out of the SDK and document that callers must pre-verify content hashes; or (c) rename the check to 'artifact_id_hash_binding' and document explicitly that this only binds the ID, not the content. Option (c) plus a hard rename of CRYPTOGRAPHIC_SECURITY_POSTURE is the minimum.

EVIDENCE:
let computed = deterministic_hash(artifact_id);  // SHA256("verifier_sdk_v1:" || artifact_id)
let hash_match = if self.config.require_hash_match {
    crate::security::constant_time::ct_eq(&computed, &request.artifact_hash)
} else { true };
evidence.push(EvidenceEntry { check_name: "hash_match".to_string(), passed: hash_match, ...});

deterministic_hash impl (lines 250-256):
fn deterministic_hash(data: &str) -> String {
    let mut hasher = Sha256::new();
    hasher.update(b"verifier_sdk_v1:");
    hasher.update(data.as_bytes());
    hex::encode(hasher.finalize())
}

notes:
HTTP CLIENT: The --deep flag requires an HTTP client to query npm registry API. Use ureq (add ureq = "2" to dependencies) — it is blocking (no async needed), pure Rust, and compatible with #![forbid(unsafe_code)].

The npm registry API endpoint is: https://registry.npmjs.org/{package-name}
Response includes: versions, time (publish dates), maintainers, dist (integrity hashes).

For --audit, use the OSV API: POST https://api.osv.dev/v1/query with body:
{"package": {"name": "package-name", "ecosystem": "npm"}}

Both APIs are public, no authentication needed. Set User-Agent header to franken-node/{version}.

