FILE: crates/franken-node/src/control_plane/fleet_transport.rs:979-981 (fleet_action_compaction_root_key) and 1000-1010 (lock_fleet_action_compaction_process)

FINDING: fleet_action_compaction_root_key(root_dir) returns root_dir.to_string_lossy().into_owned() with no canonicalization. The in-process registry (fleet_action_compaction_registry, line 974-977) uses this string as the key. Two callers within the same process that pass equivalent-but-differently-spelled paths (relative vs absolute, with/without trailing slash, with intermediate symlinks, or with .. segments) get DIFFERENT keys -> DIFFERENT registry slots -> the BTreeSet contention check incorrectly returns Ok for both. The supposed serializer (lock_fleet_action_compaction_process at line 997) is then bypassed for that pair of callers.

Compounding hazard: Rust std File::lock uses flock(2) on Linux which is per-OFD. publish_action() at line 1372 and compact_action_log_if_needed() at line 1059 both rely on (1) the in-process compaction-registry insert, (2) the file flock on the compaction lock file, (3) the file flock on shared_state, and (4) the file flock on the actions log. If (1) is bypassed via key mismatch, two threads in the same process can each open distinct fds for the compaction lock file and acquire flock-exclusive on each. The same per-OFD bypass extends to (3) and (4). Result: concurrent publish_action calls in the same process can interleave writes to the actions log and concurrent compaction can race with publish, allowing partially-written entries or compaction to drop newly published actions whose fsync ordering is not preserved.

SCENARIO:
1. Thread A: layout_a.root_dir() = "/data/fleet". publish_action -> registry insert("/data/fleet") succeeds, flock(compaction_lock fd1) ok, flock(shared_state fd1) ok, flock(actions fd1) ok, write+fsync action_v1.
2. Thread B (same process): layout_b.root_dir() = "./data/fleet" (relative). publish_action -> registry insert("./data/fleet") succeeds (different key), flock(compaction_lock fd2) ok (per-OFD), flock(shared_state fd2) ok, flock(actions fd2) ok, write+fsync action_v2.
3. Both threads append to the actions JSONL file. Without single-writer serialization, file::write_all calls from both threads can interleave at the byte level -- one thread's payload + b"\n" can be split across the other thread's bytes, producing a corrupt JSONL line that parse_jsonl_records will later reject (or worse, mis-parse).

SUGGESTED FIX:
- Canonicalize root_dir in fleet_action_compaction_root_key, falling back to a stable normalization (e.g., parent canonicalize + final segment) if canonicalize fails.
- For per-OFD flock bypass: gate publish_action and compact_action_log_if_needed behind a single per-process Mutex<()> looked up by the canonicalized key, held for the entire duration of the file operations -- not just a try-insert into a BTreeSet.
- Replace the try-insert-or-fail pattern in lock_fleet_action_compaction_process with a real Mutex acquisition that BLOCKS rather than returning lock_contention error, so concurrent publishers serialize cleanly inside one process.

EVIDENCE:
crates/franken-node/src/control_plane/fleet_transport.rs:974-1011
```
fn fleet_action_compaction_registry() -> &'static Mutex<BTreeSet<String>> {
    static REGISTRY: OnceLock<Mutex<BTreeSet<String>>> = OnceLock::new();
    REGISTRY.get_or_init(|| Mutex::new(BTreeSet::new()))
}
fn fleet_action_compaction_root_key(root_dir: &Path) -> String {
    root_dir.to_string_lossy().into_owned()   // <-- no canonicalize, equivalent paths split
}
fn lock_fleet_action_compaction_process(...) -> Result<...> {
    ...
    if !registry.insert(root_key.clone()) {
        return Err(FleetTransportError::lock_contention(...));   // <-- try-lock semantics, not blocking
    }
    Ok(FleetActionCompactionGuard { root_key })
}
```

FILE: crates/franken-node/src/control_plane/divergence_gate.rs

PATTERN:
DivergenceAuthorization construction (L280-315) and verification (L318-350) both build a canonical String via `format!` *just to feed the bytes into a hasher*:

L285-294:
```
let canonical = format!(
    "{}:{}|{}|{}|{}:{}",
    operator_id.len(), operator_id,
    resync_checkpoint_epoch, timestamp,
    reason.len(), reason
);
hasher.update(canonical.as_bytes());
```

L327-336 mirror image in `verify`. Each call allocates an intermediate heap String just to dump bytes through it. Sha256's `update()` accepts arbitrary `&[u8]` — there's no need to materialize the joined string.

IMPACT: Modest per-call but on a verification path that runs under attack scenarios (every divergence claim must verify) — every operator authorization check pays one heap alloc + format. Multiplied by replicate verification across nodes, this is wasted heap traffic.

SUGGESTED FIX:
Replace the intermediate format! with direct length-prefixed updates (a pattern already used elsewhere in this codebase, e.g. anti_entropy.rs L407-431):

```
hasher.update((operator_id.len() as u64).to_le_bytes());
hasher.update(operator_id.as_bytes());
hasher.update(b"|");
hasher.update(resync_checkpoint_epoch.to_le_bytes());
hasher.update(b"|");
hasher.update(timestamp.to_le_bytes());
hasher.update(b"|");
hasher.update((reason.len() as u64).to_le_bytes());
hasher.update(reason.as_bytes());
```

Removes the heap String, removes UTF-8 encoding overhead of format!, preserves domain separation.

EVIDENCE:
- L283-294 sign-side format!+update
- L325-336 verify-side format!+update (mirror)
- 57 format! sites in this file (rg --count) — many similar allocate-then-hash patterns may exist

FILE: crates/franken-node/src/control_plane/control_lane_policy.rs:527-531

FINDING: ControlLanePolicy::verify_all_assigned() is documented as enforcing INV-CLP-LANE-ASSIGNED ('every task class has a lane assignment') but its implementation is literally:
    pub fn verify_all_assigned(&self) -> bool {
        // With array implementation, all task classes are always assigned
        true
    }

WHY IT'S A BUG: The function exists to verify an invariant at runtime, but after the bd-17nu4 refactor that swapped BTreeMap<ControlTaskClass, LaneAssignment> for [LaneAssignment; 19], the type system makes the invariant unfalsifiable — so the runtime check became vacuous. That is technically correct only as long as the array length stays in lock-step with ControlTaskClass::all().len(). If a future refactor adds a new ControlTaskClass variant without bumping the array literal, the array initializer will fail to compile (good) — but if the variant count is altered indirectly (e.g. via a const), nothing here will catch it. More importantly, the dead test test_verify_all_assigned_rejects_missing_assignment (line 1081) calls policy.assignments.remove(&...) on an array — code that cannot compile. The test only survives because crates/franken-node/Cargo.toml sets test = false on [lib] and [[bin]], so no #[cfg(test)] block in the binary crate is ever compiled. The combination of (a) a tautological invariant check, (b) a test that pretends to falsify it but cannot compile, and (c) a build config that hides the breakage is misleading.

SUGGESTED FIX: Either (a) make verify_all_assigned a static_assert (compile-time const evaluation that ControlTaskClass::all().len() == ASSIGNMENTS.len()), removing the function entirely; or (b) keep it but actually iterate ControlTaskClass::all() and confirm each as_index() < self.assignments.len() AND self.assignments[idx].lane is one of the canonical lanes. Companion fix: enable tests for the lib (or move tests into a separate crates/franken-node/tests/ directory) so dead test code is detected.

EVIDENCE:
527: /// Check that every task class has a lane assignment (INV-CLP-LANE-ASSIGNED).
528: pub fn verify_all_assigned(&self) -> bool {
529:     // With array implementation, all task classes are always assigned
530:     true
531: }

390: assignments: [LaneAssignment; 19],

1081: fn test_verify_all_assigned_rejects_missing_assignment() {
1082:     let mut policy = ControlLanePolicy::new();
1083:     policy
1084:         .assignments
1085:         .remove(&ControlTaskClass::CancellationHandler);  // does not compile against [LaneAssignment; 19]

Cargo.toml [lib]/[[bin]] all have test = false, so this dead test is never type-checked.

