```rust
fn encode_scope_entries<'a>(entries: impl IntoIterator<Item = &'a str>) -> String {
    let mut encoded = String::new();           // String allocation
    for entry in entries {                     // Loop over operations/endpoints
        let entry_len = u64::try_from(entry.len()).unwrap_or(u64::MAX);
        encoded.push_str(&entry_len.to_string());  // to_string() per entry + push_str
        encoded.push(':');
        encoded.push_str(entry);               // push_str + potential reallocation
        encoded.push('|');
    }
    encoded                                    // Return String
}

fn scope_fingerprint(scope: &RemoteScope) -> String {
    let operations = encode_scope_entries(...);   // String allocation
    let endpoints = encode_scope_entries(...);    // Another String allocation  
    format!("ops={operations};endpoints={endpoints}") // Third String allocation
}
```

**After (pre-sized single allocation)**:
```rust
fn encode_scope_entries_to_writer<W: fmt::Write>(
    writer: &mut W, 
    entries: impl IntoIterator<Item = &str>
) -> fmt::Result {
    for entry in entries {
        let entry_len = u64::try_from(entry.len()).unwrap_or(u64::MAX);
        write!(writer, "{entry_len}:{entry}|").unwrap();  // No intermediate allocations
    }
    Ok(())
}

fn scope_fingerprint(scope: &RemoteScope) -> String {
    let est_size = scope.operations().len() * 20 + 
                   scope.endpoint_prefixes().iter().map(|s| s.len()).sum::<usize>() + 50;
    let mut buf = String::with_capacity(est_size);
    buf.push_str("ops=");
    encode_scope_entries_to_writer(&mut buf, scope.operations().iter().map(|e| e.as_str()));
    buf.push_str(";endpoints=");
    encode_scope_entries_to_writer(&mut buf, scope.endpoint_prefixes().iter().map(String::as_str));
    buf
}
```

**Impact**: (N + M + 3) String allocations → 1 pre-sized allocation (N=operations, M=endpoints)

# Task T8 — Round-3 measurement: author vef Criterion benches

Parent: bd-98xo5 (epic). NOT an optimization task — fills the
round-2 measurement gap so vef hotspots can be ranked.

## Background

Round-2 attempt to profile `vef::proof_generator` via the existing
`proof_generator_timeout_race` test binary failed: the test
finishes in 0 ms, so 1000-loop perf runs sample fork/exec overhead
(`memset_orig` 10.5 %, `set_pte_range` 3.39 %, etc.) instead of
user code. NO user-code symbol made it above 0.3 % of cycles —
see `tests/artifacts/perf/20260520T231041Z_franken_node_perf_r2/profiles/proof_generator.perf.flat.txt`.

## Static read summary

`crates/franken-node/src/vef/proof_generator.rs:242-260` —
`TestProofBackend::compute_proof_bytes`:

```rust
fn compute_proof_bytes(&self, entries: &[ReceiptChainEntry]) -> Vec<u8> {
    let mut hasher = Sha256::new();
    hasher.update(b"proof_generator_hash_v1:");
    hasher.update(b"proof-backend-v1:");
    hasher.update(u64::try_from(entries.len()).unwrap_or(u64::MAX).to_le_bytes());
    for entry in entries {
        hasher.update(u64::try_from(entry.chain_hash.len()).unwrap_or(u64::MAX).to_le_bytes());
        hasher.update(entry.chain_hash.as_bytes());
    }
    hasher.finalize().to_vec()
}
```

This function looks well-written (length-prefixed updates, one
Sha256 per batch, no String formatting on the hot path). The
round-1 static-read flag about `format!("sha256:{}", hex::encode(...))`
turned out to refer to `hash_bytes()` at L262-268, which is called
rarely (receipt ID materialisation).

So the round-3 question is: what's the actual cost of
`compute_proof_bytes` and `receipt_chain::verify_integrity` at
production scale (10 entries? 1 000? 100 000?), and is there
anything to optimise once we can see real per-entry numbers?

## Recommended bench shape

New file: `crates/franken-node/benches/vef_proof_chain_bench.rs`.
Mirror the `crypto_scheme_bench.rs` shape:

```rust
fn bench_compute_proof_bytes(c: &mut Criterion) {
    let mut group = c.benchmark_group("vef_proof_generator");
    group.sample_size(50);
    for n_entries in [16usize, 256, 4096] {
        let entries = build_chain(n_entries); // ReceiptChainEntry with realistic 64-byte chain_hash
        group.bench_with_input(BenchmarkId::new("compute_proof_bytes", n_entries),
            &entries, |b, e| b.iter(|| {
                let backend = TestProofBackend::default();
                black_box(backend.compute_proof_bytes(black_box(e)))
            }));
    }
    group.finish();
}

fn bench_verify_integrity(c: &mut Criterion) {
    let mut group = c.benchmark_group("vef_receipt_chain");
    for chain_len in [16usize, 256, 4096] {
        let chain = build_signed_chain(chain_len);
        group.bench_with_input(BenchmarkId::new("verify_integrity", chain_len),
            &chain, |b, c| b.iter(|| {
                black_box(verify_integrity(black_box(c)))
            }));
    }
    group.finish();
}
```

Register the bench in `crates/franken-node/Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[[bench]]
name = "vef_proof_chain_bench"
harness = false
```

## Acceptance

A single Criterion run produces wall-time numbers across N entries.
The hotspot ranking can then place vef::proof_generator in (or out
of) the optimisation queue with measured data instead of static
reads.

## Out of scope

- Actually optimising anything in vef. If the bench shows a
  hotspot, file a new bead for the optimisation. If it doesn't,
  document "vef proof chain is fast; no optimisation needed at
  current scale" in `tests/artifacts/perf/HISTORY.md`.

In 6d9e1b6d evidence_ledger.rs verify_evidence_entry() function lines 194-198:

```rust
let signature_bytes = hex::decode(&entry.signature).map_err(|e| {
    LedgerError::SignatureInvalid {
        reason: format!("invalid hex signature: {}", e),
    }
})?;
```

VULNERABILITY: Attacker can provide extremely long hex signature strings causing:
1. **Memory exhaustion**: hex::decode() allocates Vec<u8> of size signature.len()/2 without bounds checking
2. **CPU DoS**: Processing very long hex strings consumes excessive CPU

ATTACK SCENARIO: Entry with signature = "41".repeat(100_000_000) causes 50MB allocation + CPU burn.

FIX: Add length validation before hex::decode():
```rust
if entry.signature.len() > 128 { // Ed25519 signatures are 64 bytes = 128 hex chars
    return Err(LedgerError::SignatureInvalid { reason: "signature too long" });
}
```

IMPACT: Medium - DoS via resource exhaustion in signature validation.

